1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,112
In 793, monks in the north of England wrote
that fiery dragons were seen in the sky.

2
00:00:07,112 --> 00:00:10,419
A few months later, something
far worse came out of the sea.

3
00:00:11,719 --> 00:00:14,969
Within a hundred years, kingdom
after kingdom had fallen.

4
00:00:14,969 --> 00:00:19,760
And the last free English king was hiding
in a swamp, with only a small band of men.

5
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:23,927
Sky. Egg. They. You say
these words every single day.

6
00:00:23,927 --> 00:00:29,891
And you say them because of what happened next: a war
that decided whether English would survive at all.

7
00:00:31,191 --> 00:00:33,755
How War Made English. Part two. The Northmen.

8
00:00:33,755 --> 00:00:36,736
Raiders, settlers, and the
words they left behind.

9
00:00:38,036 --> 00:00:41,936
In part one, we saw the Angles and
Saxons build their own kingdoms.

10
00:00:41,936 --> 00:00:46,942
By the late 700s, four of them mattered
most. Northumbria. Mercia. East Anglia.

11
00:00:46,942 --> 00:00:51,773
And Wessex. They often fought each other,
but they shared one language: Old English.

12
00:00:53,073 --> 00:00:57,990
On a small island off the coast of Northumbria
stood the monastery of Lindisfarne.

13
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:02,667
It was one of the holiest places in
England, rich with books, gold and silver.

14
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:03,867
And it had no walls.

15
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:10,265
An old English record, the Anglo-Saxon
Chronicle, describes that year with these words.

16
00:01:10,265 --> 00:01:15,597
Immense whirlwinds and flashes of lightning,
and fiery dragons were seen flying in the air.

17
00:01:16,897 --> 00:01:18,023
Then came the ships.

18
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:23,481
On a summer day, usually dated the eighth of June,
raiders from Scandinavia landed on the island.

19
00:01:23,481 --> 00:01:27,307
They killed monks, smashed the
church, and carried off its treasure.

20
00:01:28,607 --> 00:01:34,638
The English scholar Alcuin of York, a teacher at the
court of Charlemagne, wrote to the king of Northumbria.

21
00:01:34,638 --> 00:01:39,720
Never before has such terror appeared in Britain
as we have now suffered from a pagan race.

22
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:42,308
Who were these raiders?

23
00:01:42,308 --> 00:01:45,893
They came from the lands we now
call Norway, Denmark and Sweden.

24
00:01:45,893 --> 00:01:50,485
In their language, Old Norse, the men who
went raiding by sea were called vikings.

25
00:01:50,485 --> 00:01:52,389
That is where our word comes from.

26
00:01:53,689 --> 00:01:57,044
Here is a surprise. Old Norse
and Old English were cousins.

27
00:01:57,044 --> 00:02:01,441
Both came from the same Germanic family,
and many everyday words were close.

28
00:02:01,441 --> 00:02:07,341
Scholars think a Viking and an English farmer could
probably understand each other, at least a little.

29
00:02:08,641 --> 00:02:11,900
For the next seventy years,
the raids came again and again.

30
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,379
Fast ships, sudden attacks, and
then gone, back across the sea.

31
00:02:15,379 --> 00:02:18,859
Monasteries and river towns
learned to fear the sight of sails.

32
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:23,676
Then, in 865, everything changed.

33
00:02:23,676 --> 00:02:25,738
A huge army landed in East Anglia.

34
00:02:25,738 --> 00:02:28,346
The Chronicle simply calls it a great army.

35
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:31,258
Historians today call it
the Great Heathen Army.

36
00:02:31,258 --> 00:02:33,138
These men had not come to raid.

37
00:02:33,138 --> 00:02:34,472
They had come to stay.

38
00:02:35,772 --> 00:02:38,387
The great army took horses
from the East Angles.

39
00:02:38,387 --> 00:02:42,256
Then it marched north, toward the
richest city in the north of England.

40
00:02:43,556 --> 00:02:47,554
In November, 866, the
great army captured York.

41
00:02:47,554 --> 00:02:50,595
The Northmen made it their
base, and called it Jorvik.

42
00:02:50,595 --> 00:02:54,029
Say it quickly, and you can
almost hear the modern name. York.

43
00:02:55,329 --> 00:02:57,514
Then the other kingdoms began to fall.

44
00:02:57,514 --> 00:02:59,986
Northumbria in 866.

45
00:02:59,986 --> 00:03:04,068
In 869, King Edmund of East Anglia was killed.

46
00:03:04,068 --> 00:03:09,472
And in 874, King Burgred of Mercia
was driven out, and fled abroad.

47
00:03:10,772 --> 00:03:13,179
Only Wessex was left. Northumbria, gone.

48
00:03:13,179 --> 00:03:15,092
East Anglia, gone. Mercia, gone.

49
00:03:15,092 --> 00:03:18,856
Of all the English kingdoms,
Wessex alone was still standing.

50
00:03:20,156 --> 00:03:23,235
The war reached Wessex in 871.

51
00:03:23,235 --> 00:03:26,810
That year, the West Saxons fought
nine battles against the Danes.

52
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,669
One of them was at Ashdown,
high on the chalk hills.

53
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:36,628
In the middle of that terrible year, the king of
Wessex died, and his younger brother took the crown.

54
00:03:36,628 --> 00:03:38,253
He was in his early twenties.

55
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:39,373
His name was Alfred.

56
00:03:40,673 --> 00:03:42,979
For a few years, there was an uneasy peace.

57
00:03:42,979 --> 00:03:51,936
Then, in the middle of winter, 878, just after Twelfth Night, the Danish
leader Guthrum launched a surprise attack on Alfred at Chippenham.

58
00:03:53,236 --> 00:03:55,449
Alfred escaped with a small band of men.

59
00:03:55,449 --> 00:04:00,760
He withdrew into the marshes of Somerset, where
he later built a fort on the island of Athelney.

60
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:05,075
For a few months, the whole future of
the English language hid there with him.

61
00:04:06,375 --> 00:04:08,560
There is a famous story from this time.

62
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:13,492
Alfred, hiding in a poor woman's house, was
asked to watch her cakes, and let them burn.

63
00:04:13,492 --> 00:04:14,556
It is a good story.

64
00:04:14,556 --> 00:04:20,048
But it was first written down about a hundred years
after Alfred died, so it is probably a legend.

65
00:04:21,348 --> 00:04:23,523
In May, Alfred came out of the marshes.

66
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:28,877
At a place called Egbert's Stone, the men of
Somerset, Wiltshire and Hampshire rode to join him.

67
00:04:30,177 --> 00:04:32,702
Two days later, the two
armies met at Edington.

68
00:04:32,702 --> 00:04:37,429
The English stood close together in a shield
wall, and fought the Danes for a long time.

69
00:04:38,729 --> 00:04:40,081
And then, the Danes broke.

70
00:04:40,081 --> 00:04:45,021
Alfred chased them back to their camp, and
surrounded it for fourteen days, until they gave in.

71
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:47,739
Guthrum agreed to peace.

72
00:04:47,739 --> 00:04:55,835
About three weeks later, he was baptised as a Christian, with Alfred
himself as his godfather, and he took a new English name: Athelstan.

73
00:04:57,135 --> 00:04:59,223
Later, the two kings agreed a border.

74
00:04:59,223 --> 00:05:04,866
It ran up the River Thames, up the River Lea, and
on toward an old Roman road called Watling Street.

75
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:06,785
The south and west stayed English.

76
00:05:06,785 --> 00:05:10,509
The north and east were Danish,
and came to be called the Danelaw.

77
00:05:11,809 --> 00:05:14,114
Alfred knew the Danes could come back.

78
00:05:14,114 --> 00:05:22,304
So he built a network of fortified towns across Wessex, places like
Winchester, Wallingford and Oxford, where people could find safety.

79
00:05:22,304 --> 00:05:25,397
In Old English, a fortified
town was called a burh.

80
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:28,234
And that word never left us.

81
00:05:28,234 --> 00:05:30,209
Burh became the modern word borough.

82
00:05:30,209 --> 00:05:35,421
Another form of it, bury, still ends many English
place names, like Canterbury and Shaftesbury.

83
00:05:36,721 --> 00:05:38,161
A king who chose English.

84
00:05:38,161 --> 00:05:41,039
That is how Alfred fought
a different kind of war.

85
00:05:41,039 --> 00:05:44,378
In his time, most learning in
Europe was written in Latin.

86
00:05:44,378 --> 00:05:47,429
But Alfred wanted books in
his people's own language.

87
00:05:48,729 --> 00:05:52,370
He wanted free young men to learn
to read English writing well.

88
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:57,686
Important Latin books were translated into
English, among them Pope Gregory's Pastoral Care.

89
00:05:57,686 --> 00:06:04,216
And around the 890s, the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle was
begun: a history of the English, written in English.

90
00:06:05,516 --> 00:06:09,094
But north of the border, the Danes
did not go home. They settled.

91
00:06:09,094 --> 00:06:14,965
They farmed, traded and married into English families,
and their children grew up hearing both languages.

92
00:06:16,265 --> 00:06:19,680
Their villages are still on the
map. Derby. Whitby. Grimsby.

93
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:27,528
That ending, by, is Old Norse for a farm or a village, and by common
counts, more than six hundred English place names end with it.

94
00:06:28,828 --> 00:06:33,989
In York, the old Viking city, many streets
are still called gate. Coppergate. Micklegate.

95
00:06:33,989 --> 00:06:38,320
In Old Norse, gata meant street, so
Micklegate simply means great street.

96
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,786
And their words moved into English.

97
00:06:41,786 --> 00:06:44,818
Sky, which first meant cloud.
Egg. Knife. Skin. Ugly.

98
00:06:44,818 --> 00:06:48,222
Husband, from the Norse word
for the master of a house.

99
00:06:48,222 --> 00:06:50,883
Even window, from vindauga, the wind's eye.

100
00:06:52,183 --> 00:06:54,457
Even the word law came from Old Norse.

101
00:06:54,457 --> 00:06:58,645
It entered English in the land ruled
by Danish law, and it never left.

102
00:06:59,945 --> 00:07:02,291
The Norse even changed the smallest words.

103
00:07:02,291 --> 00:07:03,966
Old English said hie for they.

104
00:07:03,966 --> 00:07:07,875
The Norse word they took its place,
and later them and their followed.

105
00:07:07,875 --> 00:07:10,444
You probably say them hundreds of times a day.

106
00:07:10,444 --> 00:07:12,957
Every time, you are speaking a little Viking.

107
00:07:14,257 --> 00:07:16,011
Sometimes, English kept both.

108
00:07:16,011 --> 00:07:17,824
Shirt is the Old English word.

109
00:07:17,824 --> 00:07:19,396
Skirt is its Norse cousin.

110
00:07:19,396 --> 00:07:24,112
They began as the same word, and ended
up as two different pieces of clothing.

111
00:07:25,412 --> 00:07:26,956
This mixing took centuries.

112
00:07:26,956 --> 00:07:30,616
Around 1490, the printer
William Caxton told a story.

113
00:07:30,616 --> 00:07:34,848
A merchant asked a woman for eggs, and
she said she could speak no French.

114
00:07:34,848 --> 00:07:38,851
Another man asked for eyren, the old
English word, and she understood.

115
00:07:40,151 --> 00:07:47,278
Many scholars believe this contact also helped English lose
many of its old word endings, and made its grammar simpler.

116
00:07:47,278 --> 00:07:49,074
Experts still debate how much.

117
00:07:49,074 --> 00:07:52,608
But in the Danelaw, two languages
were slowly becoming one.

118
00:07:53,908 --> 00:07:57,389
About a hundred years after
Alfred, the Vikings came back.

119
00:07:57,389 --> 00:08:02,611
In 991, at Maldon in Essex, a
Viking army defeated the English.

120
00:08:03,911 --> 00:08:08,879
England's king, Ethelred, began paying the
Vikings huge amounts of silver to go away.

121
00:08:08,879 --> 00:08:11,334
These payments were later called Danegeld.

122
00:08:11,334 --> 00:08:14,491
And each time he paid, the
Vikings came back for more.

123
00:08:15,791 --> 00:08:18,630
History remembers him as Ethelred the Unready.

124
00:08:18,630 --> 00:08:20,852
But unready did not mean unprepared.

125
00:08:20,852 --> 00:08:26,840
It comes from the Old English unraed, bad counsel,
a joke on his name, which meant noble counsel.

126
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:33,856
In 1002, Ethelred ordered the killing of Danish
men living in England, on Saint Brice's Day.

127
00:08:33,856 --> 00:08:35,197
It did not end the war.

128
00:08:35,197 --> 00:08:36,480
It only made it worse.

129
00:08:37,780 --> 00:08:40,245
The Danes answered with a full invasion.

130
00:08:40,245 --> 00:08:44,190
In 1013, Sweyn Forkbeard,
king of Denmark, took England.

131
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:49,491
He died a few weeks later, but in 1016
his son Cnut became king of all England.

132
00:08:50,791 --> 00:08:54,123
Cnut went on to rule Denmark,
and later Norway as well.

133
00:08:54,123 --> 00:08:58,000
England had become part of a
Viking empire across the North Sea.

134
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:02,055
Danish kings ruled England until 1042.

135
00:09:02,055 --> 00:09:07,917
Then the crown came back to the old English royal
family, with Edward, later known as the Confessor.

136
00:09:09,217 --> 00:09:10,475
You still speak Viking.

137
00:09:10,475 --> 00:09:14,357
Two and a half centuries of war had
left England with a mixed language.

138
00:09:14,357 --> 00:09:18,731
Every time you say sky, egg, they or law,
the Northmen are speaking through you.

139
00:09:20,031 --> 00:09:23,656
But there is one more twist in this
story, and it begins in France.

140
00:09:24,956 --> 00:09:31,686
Across the Channel, around 911, a Viking leader
named Rollo was given land in northern France.

141
00:09:31,686 --> 00:09:34,343
It became Normandy, the land of the Northmen.

142
00:09:34,343 --> 00:09:36,645
In time, his people would speak French.

143
00:09:37,945 --> 00:09:42,100
In 1066, two armies would sail
for England in the same autumn.

144
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:45,026
One came from Norway. The
other came from Normandy.

145
00:09:45,026 --> 00:09:46,840
And only one of them would win.

146
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:52,661
Next time, in part three: 1066, the
year that brought French into English.

